1. Introduction

1.1 The Problem

A clinical continuity system is a medical embedded system that acquires, stores, transports, and acts upon clinical events across a network of terminals, monitors, and remote services. Its purpose is to preserve the continuity of clinical care across failures: network outages, power loss, clock drift, key compromise, terminal destruction, and the ordinary churn of hospital infrastructure.

Systems of this class fail in ways that are not computational. They fail because a transport acknowledgement is mistaken for a clinical acknowledgement; because a measurement of unknown provenance is displayed as current; because a locally validated decision is overwritten by a remote state that is causally posterior but clinically less informed; because a suggestion produced by a decision-support model is executed as if it were a validated order. These are not bugs in the ordinary sense. They are type confusions between objects of different nature, made interchangeable in appearance by their cohabitation in a single channel, a single identifier, or a single clock.

The Therac-25 accidents of 1985–1987 established the canonical precedent. The device replaced mechanical interlocks with software enforcement and assigned an arbitrary probability of 10^{-11} to a failure mode that subsequently occurred. The software was developed by a single person over several years with little documentation, limited specifications, and no test plan. The machine has been characterized as a system trying to satisfy a number of properties without ever formally stating what those properties are. The investigation concluded that the manufacturer did not consider the software design during its assessment of failure modes, focusing on hardware and asserting the software was free of bugs.

The Therac-25 lesson is not that software is unreliable. It is that the failure modes of a clinical system must themselves be objects of specification. A system that specifies only its correct behavior has not specified its resilience. It has specified its aspirations.

1.2 The Thesis

This thesis defends a single proposition:

A resilient health system is a system whose failure modes are themselves specified, executable, and auditably traceable.

The object of proof is displaced. The question is no longer whether a system is reliable in the aggregate, but whether its failures are identifiable, localizable, and traceable before they become clinical.

The thesis is normative. It specifies what a system must satisfy to be considered resilient in this sense. It does not report an empirical result, an engineering deployment, or a clinical trial. Its value lies in the falsifiability of its claims, not in any claim of validation. This is stated once, here, and applies throughout.

1.3 The Three Founding Disciplines

Three disciplines organize the work.

Separation of orders. Transmission priority and clinical urgency constitute two distinct partial orders. They are related by a monotone non-injective morphism \varphi: (\mathcal{C}, \prec_{\text{clin}}) \to (\mathcal{T}, \preceq_{\text{trans}}). Any identification between them is a specification fault. The morphism admits no right inverse, so no layer downstream of the clinical safety core can reconstruct clinical urgency from transmission priority alone. The information loss is deliberate and irreversible, compensated by an explicit clinical urgency field signed by L4.

Refutation before affirmation. Each property is expressed as a falsifiable hypothesis, equipped with an independent oracle. No property is declared guaranteed before having survived an organized attempt at refutation. The verdict classification distinguishes four outcomes: property verified, property violated with a reproducible counter-example, inconclusive with no violation found, and inconclusive with violating behaviors possible. The distinction between "satisfied in the model" and "proven" is essential. Ten million successful tests do not constitute an exhaustive proof if the system retains unexplored behaviors.

Causal traceability. Every validated decision possesses a chain of provenance reconstructible offline, without dependence on the process that produced it. The chain is verifiable by an independent oracle holding trust keys. The property is decomposed into four separately verified sub-properties: integrity, exhaustiveness, authenticity, and clinical validity.

1.4 The Method Contract

The work is theoretical. No experiment, no physical test, and no simulation campaign is reported except for the adversarial runs described in Part XI, which are executed in the formal model, not on hardware. The properties stated are normative specifications, not empirical results.

The formal core is a stratified labeled transition system. Six layers — acquisition, durable write, sorting, interchange, clinical safety, and transversal services — are logical fibrations over a Cartesian product of local state spaces. Interface contracts constrain the authorized projections.

Two semantic regimes are distinguished. Untimed safety properties and interface contracts are stated over the stratified LTS and mechanized in Lean 4. Deadline-bearing liveness properties, which the untimed core cannot express, are stated over a network of timed automata and verified with UPPAAL under TCTL semantics. This split is not an aesthetic choice: a system that guarantees only "eventually acknowledged" has not guaranteed that the acknowledgement arrives before the patient deteriorates.

1.5 The Clinical Validity Boundary

Formal correctness of software does not imply clinical correctness of the system. The Therac-25 had informal requirements; its fatal race condition was a violation of the requirement that the actual position of the machine during operations must coincide with that requested by the operator. Formalizing this requirement would not have prevented the accident unless the formalization also captured the clinical reality that the operator's last valid command must govern, not the first.

SAT-MED incorporates a clinical validity oracle as a first-class component of the adversarial protocol. The oracle does not verify temporal-logic formulas. It audits the clinical provenance of the authorization rules: which guidelines were used, which populations were studied, which usage domains were assumed. The oracle is specified as a socio-technical component with separation of personnel from rule authorship, a documented disagreement-resolution protocol, and a place in the cross-compromise matrix. A formal proof of the contract that a suggestion is not a decision is necessary but not sufficient for clinical safety. The clinical validity oracle establishes whether the rules encoded in the clinical safety core are themselves valid for the intended clinical context.

1.6 Acknowledged Contributions and Their Limits

The separation of clinical urgency from transmission priority, related by a monotone non-injective morphism, formalizes a real problem. The consequence — that no layer downstream of the clinical safety core can reconstruct clinical urgency from transmission priority alone — is a structural constraint, not an implementation detail.

The bounded volatility window captures the distinction between clinical action and persistence guarantee. A vital alert triggers its local clinical procedure without waiting for all persistence operations to complete. Persistence is a guarantee of traceability, not a precondition for clinical action. This inversion of the conventional priority is correct.

The adversarial protocol, with its four-valued verdict classification, addresses the epistemic problem that ten million successful tests do not constitute an exhaustive proof if the system retains unexplored behaviors. The insistence that "satisfied in the model" must never be presented as "proven" is methodologically exemplary.

The formalization in Lean 4 and UPPAAL translates these specifications into machine-checkable form. The recovery safety theorem, the escalation transition prohibition, the acknowledgement type segregation, the audit oracle soundness theorem, and the watchdog contract are correct as stated. The formalization demonstrates that the specifications are executable for the components it covers.

The limits are equally important. The Lean 4 formalization covers a minimal instantiation: a single escalation automaton, a single audit chain, a recovery function for a single write-ahead-log record. The composite system with vector clocks over k nodes and a realistic clinical rule set is not instantiated. The claim that the framework is executable is supported by the formalization of its components, not by the execution of the composite system. This distinction must be maintained.

The vector clock reference model retains explicit vector clocks for the audit layer. For the transport layer, a bounded-width protocol with explicit truncation semantics is specified, and an equivalence test against the reference model has been executed at small k. The test is not a proof of equivalence for all k; it is evidence bounded by the explored space.

The clinical safety core compromise case is not adequately addressed by post-hoc detection. A clinical watchdog with its own contract, its own isolation requirements, and its own cross-compromise footprint provides real-time mitigation rather than post-hoc audit. The watchdog does not make clinical decisions; it raises a clinical anomaly event and, on a pre-defined critical pattern, triggers the local fallback procedure independently of the clinical safety core. The pattern set is versioned and requires independent clinical sign-off, so a compromised clinical safety core cannot poison it.

1.7 The Regulatory Frame

The regulatory analysis is developed for the United States. The FDA Quality Management System Regulation became effective February 2, 2026, amending the device current good manufacturing practice requirements in 21 CFR Part 820 and incorporating ISO 13485:2016 by reference, supplemented by FDA-specific requirements. The QMSR replaced the Quality System Regulation.

IEC 62304:2006 (amended 2015) defines the life cycle requirements for medical device software. The second edition is under development; its two-level rigor scheme is stable across drafts, and this thesis notes it without citing the second edition as a definitive requirement. IEC 62304 permits formal methods as a verification technique. The Lean 4 and UPPAAL artifacts of this thesis are verification evidence under that standard.

ISO 14971:2019 specifies risk management for medical devices and incorporates cybersecurity within its scope. IEC 81001-5-1:2021 specifies life cycle requirements for health software cybersecurity and is the missing security-engineering frame that ISO 14971 alone does not provide. The FDA premarket cybersecurity guidance, revised in 2026, recommends threat modeling, update and patch management, and a software bill of materials for devices with cybersecurity risk.

The regulatory stop criterion is: the analysis must be documented, the applicable framework must be identified, and the analysis must conclude that the system can legally be deployed for its intended use. An analysis that concludes "we cannot determine whether this is legal" is a stop condition, not a pass.

1.8 The Falsifiability Wager

The thesis stakes its value on refutation before affirmation. It would therefore be self-defeating to leave that stake unpaid. Part XI reports executed adversarial runs for four failure modes: network outage, duplicated or out-of-order messages, unacknowledged P0 alerts, and clock drift. Each run is reproducible from a seed, and each report contains the seed, configuration, trace, model version, counter-example where applicable, and oracle verdict.

One counter-example was discovered and corrected. Event-level deduplication at the durable-write layer did not prevent decision-level duplication: the same clinical decision derived from two distinct-but-equivalent events passed the event filter. The correction is a decision-idempotency invariant at the clinical safety layer, keyed on the decision rather than the event. The counter-example and its correction are documented rather than suppressed; a documented counter-example discovery-and-repair cycle is the strongest evidence that the methodology is not rhetorical.

The remaining gaps — the full composite formalization, the clinical validity oracle at scale, the vector-clock equivalence for large k, and the clinical companion protocol — are named as blocking conditions, not deferred as future work without consequence.

1.9 Structure

Part I establishes the mathematical foundations: the stratified labeled transition system, the two orders and their morphism, logical time against physical time, and the bounded volatility window. Part II refines the untimed core into a network of timed automata for deadline-bearing properties. Part III specifies the degraded-mode vector clock protocol and reports the executed equivalence test. Part IV specifies the clinical validity oracle. Part V specifies the escalation automaton and its degraded-mode protocol. Part VI specifies the clinical watchdog for the safety core. Part VII specifies the trust model, key management lifecycle, and cross-compromise matrix. Part VIII specifies the audit model with log rotation and retention. Part IX gives the reference algorithms. Part X integrates alarm fatigue and human factors. Part XI reports the adversarial protocol with executed runs. Part XII states the formal properties and contracts. Part XIII develops the U.S. regulatory frame. Part XIV states the blocking conditions. Part XV concludes.

Nothing is yet declared robust. Everything remains falsifiable. This is the epistemic position the work intends to hold.



Part I — Mathematical Foundations

1. Stratified Labeled Transition System

A SAT-MED system is a tuple

\mathcal{S} = (S, s_0, \mathrm{Act}, \to, \mathrm{Obs})

where S = S_{L0} \times S_{L1} \times S_{L2} \times S_{L3} \times S_{L4} \times S_{\text{transversal}}, s_0 \in S is the initial state, and the action set partitions as \mathrm{Act} = \mathrm{In} \uplus \mathrm{Out} \uplus \mathrm{Int} \uplus \mathrm{Fail}. The transition relation is \to \subseteq S \times \mathrm{Act} \times S, and \mathrm{Obs} : S \to \mathcal{O} is the observation function exposed to higher layers.

The layers are logical fibrations. For any action a \in \mathrm{Act}, its projection onto layer L_i is denoted \pi_i(a). The interface contracts are constraints on the authorized projections. The transition relation is left-total on non-terminal states: for every s \in S that is not a terminal state, there exists a \in \mathrm{Act} and s' \in S with (s, a, s') \in \to. A state where \mathrm{En}(s) = \varnothing and s is not designated terminal is a deadlock, treated as a specification fault.

2. Two Orders and the Clinical Validity Gap

The morphism \varphi: (\mathcal{C}, \prec_{\text{clin}}) \to (\mathcal{T}, \preceq_{\text{trans}}) satisfies

a \prec_{\text{clin}} b \implies \varphi(a) \preceq_{\text{trans}} \varphi(b),

but is not injective and admits no canonical inverse.

Observation 2.1. The morphism \varphi admits no right inverse in the category of monotone maps. If a right inverse \psi existed, \varphi would be injective, contradicting the specification that multiple clinical urgency classes share a transmission priority class.

Corollary 2.2. No layer downstream of L4 can reconstruct clinical urgency from transmission priority. The clinical urgency field must be transported explicitly and signed.

The clinical validity gap is orthogonal to this formal result. The morphism \varphi specifies information loss; it does not specify which clinical urgency classes exist, what they mean, or how they are assigned. These are clinical decisions, not formal ones, and are addressed by the clinical validity oracle (Part IV).

3. Logical Time Against Physical Time

An event is a tuple

e = (\mathrm{id}, \mathrm{vc}, t_{\text{wall}}, t_{\text{seq}}, p_{\text{clin}}, \mu),

where \mathrm{vc} \in \mathbb{N}^k is a vector clock over k nodes. The causal order is defined by

e_1 \to e_2 \iff (\mathrm{vc}(e_1) \leq \mathrm{vc}(e_2)) \wedge (\mathrm{vc}(e_1) \neq \mathrm{vc}(e_2)).

Four cases are distinguished: causal anteriority, causal posteriority, concurrency, and equality/anomaly. Equality of vector clocks is never interpreted as concurrency; it raises an IDENTITY_ANOMALY event traced in the audit log.

Proposition 3.1. Let e_1, e_2 be two events with \mathrm{vc}(e_1) = \mathrm{vc}(e_2). Then e_1 \parallel e_2 does not follow.

Proof. Concurrency requires \neg(e_1 \to e_2) \wedge \neg(e_2 \to e_1). If \mathrm{vc}(e_1) = \mathrm{vc}(e_2), both causal relations fail, so the definition would classify them as concurrent. But this is a specification anomaly, not legitimate concurrency: two distinct events cannot have identical vector clocks in a correctly functioning system. The reconciliation operator treats this case as IdentityAnomaly. \square

4. Bounded Volatility Window

For each event class, a bounded volatility window \Delta_{\text{vol}} \in \mathbb{R}^+ \cup \{\infty\} is the maximum theoretical loss under a power failure:

Class \Delta_{\text{vol}} Mechanism
P0 0 s Forced synchronous persistence
P1 \leq 200 ms Synchronous flush on power-loss detection
P2–P3 Bounded and documented Asynchronous write with WAL

Proposition 4.1. For P0 \prec P1 \prec P2 \prec P3, \Delta_{\text{vol}}(P0) \leq \Delta_{\text{vol}}(P1) \leq \Delta_{\text{vol}}(P2) \leq \Delta_{\text{vol}}(P3).

