Part II — Timed Automata Semantics

5. The Untimed Core Problem

The stratified LTS of Part I is untimed. Its LTL contracts can express "eventually" but not "within \delta milliseconds." The properties that matter clinically — \Delta_{\text{vol}}(P1) \leq 200 ms, escalation deadlines d(q), the 100 kbps bandwidth threshold, outage durations of 5, 30, or 120 minutes — are quantitative. An untimed LTL specification of escalation cannot distinguish an acknowledgement delivered within the clinical deadline from one delivered after the patient has deteriorated.

The formal claims of SAT-MED are therefore split into two semantic regimes:

Regime A (untimed safety). Properties P1, P2a, P3, P4 and contracts C1–C6 are stated over the untimed stratified LTS and verified in Lean 4 or TLA+.

Regime B (timed liveness and deadline). Properties P2b, P2c, and the escalation deadline properties are stated over a network of timed automata and verified with UPPAAL using TCTL.

6. Timed Automaton Network

Each layer L_i is refined into a timed automaton

\mathcal{A}_i = (Q_i, q_{i,0}, C_i, \Sigma_i, \to_i, I_i)

where Q_i is a finite set of locations, C_i a finite set of clocks, \Sigma_i the action alphabet, \to_i \subseteq Q_i \times \Phi(C_i) \times \Sigma_i \times 2^{C_i} \times Q_i the transition relation with guards and resets, and I_i : Q_i \to \Phi(C_i) the invariant assignment.

The network is the parallel composition

\mathcal{A} = \mathcal{A}_{L0} \parallel \mathcal{A}_{L1} \parallel \mathcal{A}_{L2} \parallel \mathcal{A}_{L3} \parallel \mathcal{A}_{L4} \parallel \mathcal{A}_{\text{watchdog}}

synchronizing on shared actions. The escalation automaton of Part V is the timed refinement of S_{L4}.

7. TCTL Properties for the Escalation Automaton

For each phase q \in \mathcal{E} with deadline d(q), the timed safety property is

\mathbf{A}\Box \bigl(\text{phase}(q) \wedge \text{deadline}(q) \text{ expired} \to \text{phase} \in \{\text{FAILED}, \text{ABSTAINED}\} \bigr),

and the timed liveness property is

\mathbf{A}\Diamond_{\leq d(q)} \bigl(\text{phase}(q) \vee \text{FAILED} \vee \text{ABSTAINED}\bigr).

The first says that no phase remains active past its deadline without transition to a terminal state. The second says that every execution reaches either the phase or a terminal state within the deadline. Together they exclude the failure mode "the system waits indefinitely in DETECTED while the patient deteriorates."

8. Deadlines and Delay Function

Each phase q has an absolute deadline d(q) = t_0 + \delta(p_{\text{clin}}, q, c), where \delta is monotone in clinical priority: P0 has shorter delays than P1, and so on. The delay function is part of the timed automaton specification, not the untimed LTS.

The failure detection mechanism is encoded as a clock invariant: the automaton may remain in phase q only while the clock x_q satisfies x_q \leq \delta(p_{\text{clin}}, q, c). When the invariant is violated, the automaton must take a transition, which leads either to the next phase or to FAILED.

9. Model-Checking Protocol

The timed network is model-checked with UPPAAL. The verification result is classified as:

· Verified: the TCTL property holds for all executions in the explored state space.
· Violated: a concrete counter-example trace is produced and must be reproducible from the reported seed.
· Inconclusive: the state space is too large to explore exhaustively under the available resources; the result is recorded as bounded.

No timed property is declared guaranteed on the basis of simulation alone. The model-checking result is the ground truth for Regime B.

