Part VI — L4 Clinical Watchdog

22. The Watchdog Authority Problem

The watchdog "does not have the authority to make clinical decisions" yet "has the authority to trigger the local fallback procedure independently of L4." Triggering a fallback procedure is a clinical act: it changes the patient's care pathway. This is precisely the boundary C3 exists to police. The watchdog crosses it without a contract governing the crossing.

23. Watchdog Contract C6

C6:

\mathbf{G}(\mathrm{WatchdogTrigger}(w) \to \mathrm{AuthorizedFallback}(w) \wedge \mathrm{Trace}(w))

The watchdog may trigger the fallback only if the fallback is authorized by a pre-defined clinical protocol, and every trigger is traced in the audit log.

24. Anomaly Patterns and Versioning

Anomaly patterns \mathcal{P}_{\text{anom}} include: more than n P0 alerts in t seconds from a single L4 instance; a P0 alert for a patient with no corresponding sensor reading; a P0 alert whose clinical urgency is inconsistent with the patient's recorded context.

The pattern set is versioned. A new pattern version requires independent clinical sign-off. If L4 is compromised, it cannot poison the pattern set because the pattern set is stored outside L4 and is signed by a key not held by L4.

25. Isolation and Verification

The watchdog runs in a separate execution context (a separate partition in a separation kernel, or a separate hardware module) with no shared memory or IPC channel with L4 except for the monitored output stream. The watchdog's code is formally verified against its specification using a method independent of the L4 verification.

26. Cross-Compromise Matrix Extension

The matrix includes a watchdog row and column. If the watchdog is compromised, the guarantees it provides are retracted, and the system falls back to the L4-only trust model with the associated limits.

