Part VII — Trust Model, Key Management, and Cross-Compromise

27. Trust Assumptions

A1 — Hardware root of trust. A TPM, secure element, or equivalent provides boot attestation and key storage.

A2 — Component identity. Each component is attested at boot with a cryptographic measurement.

A3 — Protected keys. Signing keys are non-extractable and inaccessible to software attackers. The lifecycle emphasizes secure generation, hardware-backed storage, controlled distribution, secure usage, and timely destruction.

A4 — Independent authorization policy. L4 does not self-authorize.

A5 — Verifier separation. The verification oracle shares no code, memory, or execution context with the system.

A6 — Physical independence of checkpoints. At least one checkpoint copy is on a support physically independent of the terminal.

A7 — Clinical validity of L4 rules. Validated by the clinical validity oracle. If the oracle is compromised, A7 is retracted.

28. Key Management Lifecycle

Generation. Keys are generated in an HSM or secure element with a certified RNG. Generation is auditable.

Distribution. Signing keys are distributed through a secure channel whose protocol is specified and verified.

Rotation. Keys rotate at defined intervals or on suspicion of compromise. Rotation does not invalidate past legitimate events. The transition from old to new key creates no gap in the audit chain.

Revocation. Revoked keys are propagated to all verifiers. Events signed after revocation are invalid; events signed before remain valid.

Recovery. A lost key has a recovery procedure that does not allow an attacker to forge events.

29. Cross-Compromise Matrix with Shared Dependency

L_i \backslash L_j L0 L1 L2 L3 L4 WD Oracle
L0 — cond cond full cond full full
L1 full — cond full cond full full
L2 full full — full cond full full
L3 full full full — full full full
