Part VIII — Audit Model with Log Rotation

31. Chained Log Architecture

The log is append-only and hash-chained: \mathrm{prev}(e_i) = H(e_{i-1}). A signed checkpoint contains the last hash, a monotone counter, signer identity, and timestamp.

Proposition 31.1 (Audit Correctness Soundness). A valid artifact set satisfying both the hash chain verification and the monotone counter exhaustiveness check passes the oracle.

32. Rotation and Retention

A hash chain does not allow natural rotation: multi-year retention requires either preserving the entire chain or reconstructing a new chain from scratch. The rotation policy is:

Rotation trigger. Size (1 GB) or age (30 days).

Checkpoint at rotation. A signed checkpoint contains the hash of the last record in the old log and the first record in the new log.

Archive integrity. Archived logs are on tamper-evident media with their own integrity guarantees. The chain is verifiable across archives.

Retention. Minimum five years, consistent with FDA device registration validity.

Destruction. Destruction is recorded and verified. No unexplained gap in the chain.

33. Independence and Verification

The oracle reimplements the chaining function from the format specification alone. It does not reuse writing code. It can export a self-contained checkpoint and verify it offline.

