Part X — Alarm Fatigue and Human Factors Integration

38. Alert Burden Monitoring

Clinical decision support systems generate excessive, non-contextual alerts, causing desensitization with override rates exceeding 90% in some settings. The system monitors:

\text{Burden}(c, s) = \frac{|\text{Alerts}(c, s)|}{|\text{Shift}(c, s)|}, \qquad \text{OverrideRate}(a) = \frac{|\text{Overrides}(a)|}{|\text{Displays}(a)|}.

Any alert with an override rate exceeding 70% over a 90-day review period is subject to mandatory governance review.

39. Suppression and Workflow Integration

Suppression. Conditions for suppression (known transient event) and conditions where suppression is prohibited (P0 alerts) are specified. Suppression is traced.

Workflow. The escalation automaton is aligned with clinical workflow rhythms. A P1 alert during shift change may be routed to the incoming nurse with a handover note rather than immediately escalated to the on-call physician.

Feedback loop. Clinicians can provide feedback on alert relevance. The feedback adjusts thresholds through the governance process, not automatically.

40. Clinical Companion Protocol

The framework specifies the machine side. The clinical companion protocol specifies the human side: roles, escalation chain, fallback duties, and manual procedures. It is a first-class artifact type, like the calibration protocol. Its unfilled state is a named stop condition.

The evidence base on failure to rescue shows delayed escalation occurs in 20.7–47.1% of patients and is associated with greater mortality in multiple studies, with causes including hierarchy and failures in communication. A traceability system that does not address these human factors systematically overstates what it buys.

