Part XI — Adversarial Protocol with Executed Runs

41. Adversary Model

Adversary class. The adversary is Dolev–Yao for the network (can eavesdrop, intercept, modify, and inject messages) plus Byzantine for compromised nodes (can behave arbitrarily within their capabilities). The adversary does not break cryptographic primitives or read non-extractable keys.

Capabilities. Inject faults in the Fail alphabet; reorder, delay, or drop messages; manipulate wall-clock readings; attempt to forge events or decisions; attempt to bypass authorization rules.

Limitations. Cannot violate hardware trust assumptions without a capability grant; cannot read non-extractable keys; cannot modify the verification oracle.

42. Executed Adversarial Runs

The test loop has been executed for F1, F3, F5, and F8:

F1 — Network outage. Outage of 5, 30, and 120 minutes injected. Required behavior: display local mode, queue events, trigger fallback. Result: Verified. The system displayed local mode, queued events without loss of P0/P1, and triggered fallback per deadline. No remote alert was presented as delivered without acknowledgement.

F3 — Duplicated/out-of-order messages. Duplicates injected at L1 and at L3. Result: Verified for event-level dedup at L1. Decision-level idempotency at L4 was verified separately with the idempotency key. A counter-example was found in the original event-only dedup: the same decision derived from two equivalent events passed the event filter. The correction is the decision-idempotency invariant of §36.

F5 — Unacknowledged P0 alert. P0 alert injected with acknowledgement suppressed. Result: Verified. The local fallback triggered at the deadline; the escalation traced each attempt; the system did not assimilate sending to handling.

F8 — Clock drift. Wall clock jumped backward and forward. Result: Verified. Events were not reordered based on wall-clock order; no legitimate decision was invalidated by a temporal jump.

Each run is reproducible from its seed. The reports contain seed, configuration, trace, model version, counter-example (where applicable), and oracle verdict. A single counter-example was found in F3 and corrected; this is the falsifiability discipline in practice.

43. Result Classification

Value Meaning
True Product empty: no counter-example found
False A reproducible counter-example was found
Inconclusive-true Model does not allow conclusion, but no violation found
Inconclusive-false Model does not allow conclusion, and violating behaviors are possible

