Part XV — Conclusion

This thesis claims that a clinical continuity system can be described with enough rigor to be refuted. The three founding corrections — explicit dependencies of the continuity core, an active clinical safety core, and strict separation of clinical and transmission orders — are formalized as mathematical constraints whose violation is detectable.

The central thesis holds in one sentence: a resilient health system is a system whose failure modes are themselves specified, executable, and auditably traceable.

Three boundary conditions are now integrated into the specification. The clinical validity gap: formal correctness of software does not imply clinical correctness, and the Therac-25 precedent establishes that this distinction is not academic. The vector clock degradation protocol: the reference model of explicit vector clocks is retained for the audit layer, with a bounded-width degraded mode specified for the transport layer and an executed equivalence test. The L4 compromise vulnerability: a clinical watchdog with contract C6 provides real-time anomaly detection rather than post-hoc audit.

The falsifiability wager is paid in Part XI: adversarial runs for F1, F3, F5, and F8 have been executed, one counter-example was found and corrected, and the results are reproducible from seeds. The remaining gaps — the composite formalization, the full clinical validity oracle, the vector-clock equivalence at large k — are named as blocking conditions, not deferred as future work without consequence.

The contribution of SAT-MED will not reside in the number of standards cited. It resides in the reproducible demonstration that precise properties resist precisely defined failures, and in the honest identification of those that do not resist. Nothing is yet declared robust. Everything remains falsifiable.

