Appendix B — Formal Semantics of the Stratified LTS

B.1 Transition System

The stratified LTS is a tuple \mathcal{S} = (S, s_0, \mathrm{Act}, \to, \mathrm{Obs}) where S = S_{L0} \times S_{L1} \times S_{L2} \times S_{L3} \times S_{L4} \times S_{\text{transversal}}. The transition relation \to \subseteq S \times \mathrm{Act} \times S is required to be left-total on non-terminal states: for every s \in S that is not a terminal state, there exists a \in \mathrm{Act} and s' \in S with (s, a, s') \in \to.

B.2 Projections

For i \in \{L0, L1, L2, L3, L4, \text{transversal}\}, the projection \pi_i : S \to S_{L_i} is the canonical projection of the Cartesian product. For an action a \in \mathrm{Act}, the projection \pi_i(a) is defined by the unique decomposition of a into its layer-local components. An action a is local to L_i if \pi_j(a) = \epsilon for all j \neq i, where \epsilon denotes the identity action on the layer.

B.3 Enabledness

An action a is enabled at s if (s, a, s') \in \to for some s'. The set of enabled actions at s is denoted \mathrm{En}(s). The specification requires:

\mathrm{En}(s) \neq \varnothing \iff s \text{ is not terminal}.

A state where \mathrm{En}(s) = \varnothing and s is not designated terminal is a deadlock, and is treated as a specification fault.

B.4 Executions

An execution is a finite or infinite sequence

\sigma = s_0 \xrightarrow{a_0} s_1 \xrightarrow{a_1} s_2 \xrightarrow{a_2} \cdots

with (s_k, a_k, s_{k+1}) \in \to for every k. The set of all executions is \mathrm{Exec}(\mathcal{S}). The set of maximal executions — those that are either infinite or terminate in a terminal state — is \mathrm{Exec}_{\max}(\mathcal{S}).

B.5 Layer Semantics

Each layer L_i has a local state space S_{L_i} and a local transition relation \to_{L_i} induced by the global relation restricted to local actions. The global relation is the union of local relations plus inter-layer synchronization actions, subject to the interface contracts of Appendix D.

B.6 Timed Refinement

For the deadline-bearing properties, each layer is refined into a timed automaton \mathcal{A}_i = (Q_i, q_{i,0}, C_i, \Sigma_i, \to_i, I_i) where Q_i is a finite set of locations, C_i a finite set of clocks, \Sigma_i the action alphabet, \to_i \subseteq Q_i \times \Phi(C_i) \times \Sigma_i \times 2^{C_i} \times Q_i the transition relation with guards and resets, and I_i : Q_i \to \Phi(C_i) the invariant assignment. The network is the parallel composition

\mathcal{A} = \mathcal{A}_{L0} \parallel \mathcal{A}_{L1} \parallel \mathcal{A}_{L2} \parallel \mathcal{A}_{L3} \parallel \mathcal{A}_{L4} \parallel \mathcal{A}_{\text{watchdog}}

synchronizing on shared actions. The escalation automaton of Appendix F is the timed refinement of S_{L4}.

