Appendix C — Proof Sketches of Key Structural Properties

C.1 Non-Invertibility of \varphi

Proposition C.1. The morphism \varphi : (\mathcal{C}, \prec_{\text{clin}}) \to (\mathcal{T}, \preceq_{\text{trans}}) admits no right inverse in the category of monotone maps.

Proof sketch. Assume for contradiction that a monotone map \psi : (\mathcal{T}, \preceq_{\text{trans}}) \to (\mathcal{C}, \prec_{\text{clin}}) satisfies \psi \circ \varphi = \mathrm{id}_{\mathcal{C}}. Then \varphi must be injective, since a right inverse exists only for injective maps in the category of sets. But \varphi is required to be non-injective by specification. Contradiction. \square

Corollary C.2. No layer downstream of L4 can reconstruct clinical urgency from transmission priority. The clinical urgency field must be transported explicitly and signed.

The proof is elementary. Its weight is not in its depth but in its consequence: an architectural constraint that many clinical systems violate by conflating "urgent to transmit" with "urgent to treat."

C.2 Equality of Vector Clocks Is Not Concurrency

Proposition C.3. Let e_1, e_2 be two events with \mathrm{vc}(e_1) = \mathrm{vc}(e_2). Then e_1 \parallel e_2 does not follow.

Proof sketch. Concurrency is defined as e_1 \parallel e_2 \iff \neg(e_1 \to e_2) \wedge \neg(e_2 \to e_1). Suppose \mathrm{vc}(e_1) = \mathrm{vc}(e_2). Then \mathrm{vc}(e_1) \leq \mathrm{vc}(e_2) and \mathrm{vc}(e_2) \leq \mathrm{vc}(e_1) hold component-wise. But e_1 \to e_2 requires strict inequality in at least one component, which is false. Therefore both causal relations fail, and by definition e_1 \parallel e_2 would follow. However, this is a specification anomaly, not a legitimate concurrency, because two distinct events cannot have identical vector clocks in a correctly functioning system. \square

Consequence. The reconciliation operator treats \mathrm{vc}(e_1) = \mathrm{vc}(e_2) \wedge \mathrm{id}(e_1) \neq \mathrm{id}(e_2) as IdentityAnomaly, never as concurrency.

C.3 Idempotence of Reconciliation

Proposition C.4. The reconciliation function \mathrm{Reconcile} : (L, R) \mapsto (L', R') satisfies \mathrm{Reconcile} \circ \mathrm{Reconcile} = \mathrm{Reconcile}.

Proof sketch. Apply Reconcile to (L, R) producing (L', R'). By construction, every event in L' is either identical to an event in L or an element of R that causally follows it, or a Conflict wrapper. Applying Reconcile again to (L', R') produces (L'', R''). For each event e \in L', the fusion with its image in R' produces the same result. If e was a Conflict, it remains a Conflict. Therefore L'' = L', and idempotence holds. \square

C.4 Monotonicity of the Volatility Window

Proposition C.5. For priority classes ordered by P0 \prec P1 \prec P2 \prec P3, the volatility window satisfies \Delta_{\text{vol}}(P0) \leq \Delta_{\text{vol}}(P1) \leq \Delta_{\text{vol}}(P2) \leq \Delta_{\text{vol}}(P3).

Proof sketch. By specification, P0 imposes \Delta_{\text{vol}} = 0, P1 imposes \Delta_{\text{vol}} \leq 200 ms, and P2/P3 impose bounded but larger windows. The inequality follows by construction. \square

C.5 Preservation of Truncated Causal Relation

Proposition C.6. For any two events e_1, e_2 within the truncated window of width k_{\max},

e_1 \to e_2 \iff e_1 \to_{\text{trunc}} e_2.

Proof sketch. Within the window, the truncated vector clock includes exactly the same components as the reference vector clock for the nodes involved. The Lamport clock provides a consistent total order for events at the same node. Therefore the component-wise comparison yields the same result. \square

C.6 Loss Bound for the Truncated Mode

Proposition C.7. The maximum causal information loss is bounded by

\text{Loss}(k, k_{\max}, \lambda) \leq 1 - \left(1 - \frac{k_{\max}}{k}\right)^{\lambda},

where \lambda is the churn rate (fraction of nodes leaving per time unit).

Proof sketch. The probability that an event involves a node outside the truncated window is 1 - k_{\max}/k. Over \lambda time units, the probability that at least one such event occurs is 1 - (1 - k_{\max}/k)^{\lambda}. \square

C.7 Idempotence of Decision-Level Execution

Proposition C.8. Under the decision-idempotency invariant of Appendix D.3,

\mathbf{G}(\mathrm{Decision}(d) \wedge \mathrm{alreadyExecuted}(d) \to \neg \mathrm{NewAction}(d)).

Proof sketch. The invariant is enforced by an idempotency key at the decision level. When a decision is derived from two distinct-but-equivalent events, the decision is keyed on the clinical action, not on the event. The second derivation finds the key already present and takes no new action. \square

