Appendix D — Full Contract Specifications

D.1 C1 — Creation and Persistence of an Event

Producer. L0 or L4. Consumer. L1.

Precondition. An event e is constructed with fields: unique identifier \mathrm{id}(e) and version; pseudonymized patient identifier, when relevant; source and identity of author or system; acquisition time t_{\text{acq}}(e) and recording time t_{\text{rec}}(e); assigned priority p_{\text{clin}}(e) and its justification; freshness and integrity metadata \mu(e); clinical status: observation, suggestion, validated decision, or alert.

Postcondition. An event is declared durably recorded only after confirmation of its persistence according to the retained storage model. If the write fails, the interface must not display a false acknowledgement.

Formal specification.

\mathbf{G}(\mathrm{Durable}(e) \to \mathrm{Persisted}(e)) \wedge \mathbf{G}(\neg \mathrm{Persisted}(e) \to \neg \mathrm{FalseACK}(e))

Recovery clause. Any transaction interrupted before its validation marker is treated at restart as INDETERMINATE, never as absent.

D.2 C2 — Admission into the Transmission Queue

Producer. L1. Consumer. L2 or L3.

L1 retains the event until confirmation of reception by the designated recipient, or until an explicit decision of retention or authorized deletion. L2 may compute transmission order but cannot silently modify the assigned clinical priority. If resources are insufficient, the system signals saturation and applies a validated policy.

Formal specification.

\mathbf{G}(\mathrm{Admitted}(e) \to \mathbf{F}(\mathrm{Delivered}(e) \vee \mathrm{Retained}(e) \vee \mathrm{AuthorizedDeletion}(e)))

Non-eviction clause. P0 and P1 are non-evictive. Silent removal of an urgent alert is structurally impossible.

Saturation clause. Under saturation, admission of P2/P3 is refused and an explicit loss event is recorded containing the minimal content. The loss event is traced and visible to L4.

D.3 C3 — Processing of an AI Suggestion

Producer. Local or remote AI system. Consumer. L4.

A suggestion must contain its provenance, the data used, timestamps, model version, validation domain, and available uncertainties. L4 applies authorization, freshness, and abstention rules: a suggestion never constitutes a clinical decision by default; an unvalidated suggestion cannot directly trigger an unauthorized therapeutic action; a validated clinical decision must retain the identity of its author or the pre-approved protocol that authorizes it; a stale suggestion, or one from an unvalidated context, is rejected or submitted for reevaluation.

Formal specification.

\mathbf{G}(\mathrm{Suggestion}(s) \to \neg \mathrm{Decision}(s))

Abstention clause. A suggestion outside the deployment context tuple \kappa is treated as default abstention, not as a suggestion to be reevaluated.

Decision-idempotency clause. A clinical decision d is keyed on the action, not on the event that triggered it:

\mathbf{G}(\mathrm{Decision}(d) \wedge \mathrm{alreadyExecuted}(d) \to \neg \mathrm{NewAction}(d)).

This clause corrects a defect identified in the specification: event-level deduplication at L1 does not prevent decision-level duplication when the same decision is derived from two distinct-but-equivalent events.

D.4 C4 — Clinical Escalation

Producer. L4. Consumers. Local caregiver, clinical supervisor, configured fallback mechanism.

A P0 alert immediately triggers the validated local procedure. Its remote transmission is an additional action, not a precondition. Each escalation has an identifier, an applicable deadline, the channels attempted, the acknowledgements received, and the result. Absence of acknowledgement does not mean the alert has been handled.

Formal specification.

\mathbf{G}(\mathrm{Alert}(P0) \to \mathrm{LocalProcedure}(P0))

Phase invariant. A TRANSPORT_ACK is never a REMOTE_CLINICAL_ACK. Phase transitions are ordered and no skipping is permitted, except that DETECTED may transition directly to BACKUP_CHANNEL if transport acknowledgement is not received within its deadline.

D.5 C5 — Reconciliation After Reconnection

Producer. L1. Consumer. Remote service and L4.

Reconnection must not cause repetition of a clinical decision nor disappearance of a local event. Synchronization must be idempotent, detect version conflicts, and distinguish new events from old data. A medical decision taken locally cannot be silently overwritten by a remote state.

Formal specification.

\mathbf{G}(\mathrm{Reconnected} \to \neg \mathrm{SilentLoss}) \wedge \mathbf{G}(\mathrm{Reconnected} \to \neg \mathrm{SilentOverwrite})

Conflict clause. A conflict not automatically resolvable raises a CONFLICT event traced in the audit, visible to L4, escalated according to the authority tuple \kappa.

D.6 C6 — Clinical Watchdog

Producer. Clinical watchdog \mathcal{W}. Consumer. Local fallback procedure, audit log.

The watchdog may trigger the fallback only if the fallback is authorized by a pre-defined clinical protocol, and every trigger is traced in the audit log.

Formal specification.

\mathbf{G}(\mathrm{WatchdogTrigger}(w) \to \mathrm{AuthorizedFallback}(w) \wedge \mathrm{Trace}(w))

Authority clause. The watchdog does not make clinical decisions. It raises a CLINICAL_ANOMALY event and, on a pre-defined critical pattern, triggers the local fallback procedure independently of L4.

Pattern-set clause. The anomaly pattern set is versioned. A new pattern version requires independent clinical sign-off. If L4 is compromised, it cannot poison the pattern set because the pattern set is stored outside L4 and is signed by a key not held by L4.

