Appendix E — Durable Write Protocol: Detailed Specification

E.1 Primitive Operations

The durability protocol relies on a small set of primitive operations whose semantics are assumed, not proven:

Primitive Semantics Assumption
\mathrm{append}(\text{log}, \text{record}) Appends record to log buffer Atomic at record granularity
\mathrm{fsync}(\text{log}) Forces log buffer to durable storage Returns success only when durable
\mathrm{fdatasync}(\text{log}) Forces data (not metadata) to durable storage Returns success only when durable
\mathrm{read}(\text{log}, \text{offset}) Reads record at offset Returns last durable value

The semantics are assumed relative to an explicit hardware model. The model must name the storage medium, the presence or absence of a write cache, the durability barrier used, and the failure modes considered.

E.2 Transaction Lifecycle

A transaction passes through the states PENDING, DURABLE, INDETERMINATE, and ABORTED. Transitions: PENDING → DURABLE on successful completion of the commit persistence primitive; PENDING → INDETERMINATE on interruption before commit marker durability; PENDING → ABORTED on explicit abort before commit. DURABLE is terminal. INDETERMINATE is resolved at recovery by inspection of the log. ABORTED is terminal.

E.3 Write-Ahead Log Format

Each log record has the form

\mathrm{rec} = (\mathrm{tx\_id}, \mathrm{op}, \mathrm{payload}, \mathrm{checksum})

where \mathrm{op} \in \{\mathrm{BEGIN}, \mathrm{COMMIT}, \mathrm{ABORT}\}. The checksum is computed over the record fields and verified at recovery. A record whose checksum fails is treated as not persisted.

E.4 Recovery Procedure

At startup, the recovery procedure: (1) scans the log from the last known checkpoint; (2) groups records by transaction identifier; (3) for each transaction, determines its terminal state — BEGIN and COMMIT present and checksums valid → DURABLE; BEGIN present, COMMIT absent or checksum invalid → INDETERMINATE; ABORT present → ABORTED; only COMMIT without BEGIN → log corruption, treated as INDETERMINATE with an alert; (4) rebuilds the committed state from durable transactions; (5) reports indeterminate transactions to the higher layer for clinical handling.

E.5 Power-Failure Semantics

A power failure at any point during the transaction lifecycle produces one of the following outcomes, subject to the hardware model: the BEGIN record is lost → transaction invisible, treated as never begun; the BEGIN record is durable, the COMMIT record is lost → transaction INDETERMINATE; both records durable, the commit marker is not yet acknowledged to the caller → transaction DURABLE; both records durable and acknowledged → transaction DURABLE. The specification requires that no outcome be DURABLE without the commit record being durable, and no outcome be silently absent if the BEGIN record survived.

E.6 Lean 4 Formalization (Specified)

The Lean 4 formalization defines the transaction state type, the WAL record type, the recovery function, and the theorem:

```
theorem recovery_safety_bound :
  ∀ (tx : Transaction),
    tx.state = PENDING →
    (tx.begin_valid ∧ ¬ tx.commit_valid) →
    recover tx = INDETERMINATE
```

The theorem is proved for the minimal instantiation: a single WAL record, a single transaction identifier, a single storage medium. The composite system with multiple concurrent transactions and multiple storage media is not instantiated.

