Appendix F — Escalation Automaton: Full State Space

F.1 States

\mathcal{E} = \{\mathrm{DETECTED}, \mathrm{TRANSPORT\_ACK}, \mathrm{LOCAL\_HUMAN\_ACK}, \mathrm{BACKUP\_CHANNEL}, \mathrm{REMOTE\_CLINICAL\_ACK}, \mathrm{FAILED}, \mathrm{ABSTAINED}\}.

F.2 Transition Relation

The transition relation \to_{\mathcal{E}} is a strict partial order on the first five states, with FAILED and ABSTAINED as absorbing terminal states:

\to_{\mathcal{E}} = \begin{Bmatrix}
(\mathrm{DETECTED}, \mathrm{TRANSPORT\_ACK}), \\
(\mathrm{DETECTED}, \mathrm{LOCAL\_HUMAN\_ACK}), \\
(\mathrm{DETECTED}, \mathrm{BACKUP\_CHANNEL}), \\
(\mathrm{TRANSPORT\_ACK}, \mathrm{LOCAL\_HUMAN\_ACK}), \\
(\mathrm{TRANSPORT\_ACK}, \mathrm{BACKUP\_CHANNEL}), \\
(\mathrm{LOCAL\_HUMAN\_ACK}, \mathrm{BACKUP\_CHANNEL}), \\
(\mathrm{LOCAL\_HUMAN\_ACK}, \mathrm{REMOTE\_CLINICAL\_ACK}), \\
(\mathrm{BACKUP\_CHANNEL}, \mathrm{REMOTE\_CLINICAL\_ACK}), \\
(\cdot, \mathrm{FAILED}), \\
(\cdot, \mathrm{ABSTAINED})
\end{Bmatrix}

The transition (\mathrm{DETECTED}, \mathrm{BACKUP\_CHANNEL}) corrects a defect identified in the specification: if a P0 alert's first packet is lost, the automaton must not be forced to wait for a TRANSPORT_ACK before activating the backup channel.

F.3 Deadlines

Each phase q has an absolute deadline d(q), computed from the detection time t_0:

d(q) = t_0 + \delta(p_{\text{clin}}, q, c)

where \delta is a delay function parameterized by clinical priority, phase, and care setting c. The delay function is monotone in clinical priority: P0 has shorter delays than P1, and so on.

F.4 Failure Detection

At each clock tick t, the automaton checks whether t > d(q) while the current phase is strictly less than q in the partial order. If so, the automaton transitions to FAILED and records the phase that missed its deadline. A FAILED state is traced and triggers the fallback procedure.

F.5 Acknowledgement Semantics

Each acknowledgement carries a type, an actor, and a timestamp. The type is one of: TRANSPORT_ACK (the transport layer confirms reception of the alert packet by the destination host); LOCAL_HUMAN_ACK (a local human operator confirms perception of the alert); REMOTE_CLINICAL_ACK (a remote clinical actor confirms clinical handling). The automaton enforces that these three types are never conflated. A TRANSPORT_ACK does not satisfy the deadline of REMOTE_CLINICAL_ACK.

F.6 TCTL Properties (Specified)

For each phase q, the timed safety property is

\mathbf{A}\Box \bigl(\text{phase}(q) \wedge \text{deadline}(q) \text{ expired} \to \text{phase} \in \{\text{FAILED}, \text{ABSTAINED}\} \bigr),

and the timed liveness property is

\mathbf{A}\Diamond_{\leq d(q)} \bigl(\text{phase}(q) \vee \text{FAILED} \vee \text{ABSTAINED}\bigr).

These properties are stated in TCTL for verification with UPPAAL. No verification has been executed; the properties are specifications.

F.7 Terminal Failure Mode and Clinical Protocol

The escalation automaton has FAILED and ABSTAINED as absorbing terminal states. The specification must define what happens after FAILED: what is displayed to the patient, what is recorded in the audit, what is the clinical protocol for a situation in which no electronic escalation channel has succeeded.

Display. The terminal displays a persistent red banner reading "NO ELECTRONIC ESCALATION CHANNEL AVAILABLE — FOLLOW MANUAL PROTOCOL." The display must not simulate normal operation.

Recording. Every FAILED state is recorded with the reason for failure, the channels attempted, the timestamps of each attempt, and the identity of the clinician who acknowledged the FAILED state.

Manual escalation. The specification references a clinical companion protocol developed with clinical professionals, defining whom to contact, by what means (phone, pager, in-person), and what clinical actions to take while awaiting response. The unfilled state of this protocol is a named stop condition.

System behavior after FAILED. The FAILED state is not silent. The degraded-mode indicator persists until the episode is resolved. The system does not automatically return to normal mode without explicit human confirmation.

The formal framework can specify that a FAILED state is reached; it cannot specify what a nurse should do when the monitor says FAILED and the patient is deteriorating. This is a clinical protocol specification that must be developed with clinical professionals.

