Appendix G — Vector Clock Equivalence Framework

G.1 Reference Model

The reference model uses explicit vector clocks: for each event e, \mathrm{vc}(e) \in \mathbb{N}^k where k is the number of participating nodes. Transmission cost is \Theta(k) per message. The causal relation is defined component-wise:

e_1 \to e_2 \iff (\mathrm{vc}(e_1) \leq \mathrm{vc}(e_2)) \wedge (\mathrm{vc}(e_1) \neq \mathrm{vc}(e_2)).

G.2 Limitations of the Reference Model

The limitation is structural: vector clocks cannot handle dynamic systems and the vector size is the number of communicating processes in a distributed system. At k = 100, each event carries at least 100 integers — 400 bytes if 32-bit, 800 bytes if 64-bit. For a P0 alert over a degraded network below 100 kbps, the overhead is non-negligible. The fixed index requirement imposes two restrictive conditions: the number of processes must be constant and known in advance. A hospital system with nodes joining and leaving violates both.

G.3 Degraded-Mode Protocol

The reference model retains explicit vector clocks for the audit layer, where computational cost is acceptable and causal reconstruction is essential. For the transport layer, a bounded-width vector clock with explicit truncation semantics is specified.

Definition G.1 (Truncated Vector Clock). Let k_{\max} be a fixed bound. For an event e at node n,

\mathrm{vc}_{\text{trunc}}(e) = (\mathrm{vc}(e)[n_1], \ldots, \mathrm{vc}(e)[n_{k_{\max}}], L(e))

where n_1, \ldots, n_{k_{\max}} are the k_{\max} most recently active nodes (including n), and L(e) is a Lamport clock.

Definition G.2 (Truncated Causal Relation). Within the truncated window,

e_1 \to_{\text{trunc}} e_2 \iff (\mathrm{vc}_{\text{trunc}}(e_1) \leq \mathrm{vc}_{\text{trunc}}(e_2)) \wedge (\mathrm{vc}_{\text{trunc}}(e_1) \neq \mathrm{vc}_{\text{trunc}}(e_2)).

Outside the window, no causal claim is made; the system records a TRUNCATION_LOSS event.

Definition G.3 (Mode Change Event). When the system switches between reference and truncated modes, it records a MODE_CHANGE event containing the timestamp, triggering condition, and current k.

G.4 Candidate Optimizations

Three optimizations are considered, none adopted by default:

Interval Tree Clocks (ITC). A causality tracking mechanism that generalizes both version vectors and vector clocks, designed for dynamic systems with no global identifiers and decentralized entity creation. Its stamp size stabilizes rather than growing linearly. ITC is not adopted because its use in safety-critical medical systems is not established and its equivalence to the reference model has not been verified for the clinical event patterns of interest.

Singhal–Kshemkalyani differential technique. Only components that changed since the last message are transmitted. Requires assumptions on message ordering and on the persistence of the receiver's knowledge of the sender's clock.

Fowler–Zwaenepoel dependency technique. Stores only direct dependencies. Reduces storage to the size of the direct dependency set, at the cost of an increase in transmission for indirect dependencies.

G.5 Equivalence Criterion

An optimization \mathcal{O} is equivalent to the reference model if, for every pair of events (e_1, e_2) in every execution considered,

\mathcal{O}(e_1) \to_{\mathcal{O}} \mathcal{O}(e_2) \iff \mathrm{vc}(e_1) \to \mathrm{vc}(e_2),

where \to_{\mathcal{O}} is the causal relation induced by the optimized representation. The equivalence must hold for all four cases: anteriority, posteriority, concurrency, equality/anomaly.

G.6 Test Protocol (Specified, Not Executed)

The protocol is specified as follows: (1) generate a distributed trace with events from all four cases; (2) compute the reference causal relation; (3) compute the optimized causal relation; (4) compare the two relations for exact equality; (5) measure transmission cost and storage cost; (6) document the assumptions under which equivalence holds. No optimization is adopted without a documented equivalence test. The reference model is retained as the semantic ground truth.

Epistemic status. This protocol has not been executed. No trace has been generated, no comparison has been performed, and no measurement has been taken. The protocol is a specification of how the equivalence claim would be tested; it is not evidence that the claim holds. The adoption of the truncated mode in the architecture is therefore a specification decision, not an empirically supported one. The equivalence test is a blocking condition for deployment, not a completed task.

