Appendix H — Trust Model and Cross-Compromise Analysis

H.1 Trust Assumptions

The following assumptions are named explicitly. If any fails, the corresponding guarantee falls.

A1 — Hardware root of trust. A hardware element (TPM, secure element, or equivalent) provides a root of trust for boot attestation and key storage.

A2 — Component identity. Each software component is attested at boot with a cryptographic measurement.

A3 — Protected keys. Signing keys are stored in non-extractable form, inaccessible to software attackers. The lifecycle emphasizes secure generation, hardware-backed storage, controlled distribution, secure usage, and timely destruction.

A4 — Independent authorization policy. L4 does not self-authorize. Authorization rules are defined outside L4 and cannot be modified by L4 alone.

A5 — Verifier separation. The verification oracle does not share code, memory, or execution context with the system under verification.

A6 — Physical independence of some checkpoints. At least one copy of each checkpoint is stored on a support physically independent of the terminal.

A7 — Clinical validity of L4 rules. The authorization and abstention rules encoded in L4 have been validated against clinical guidelines for the intended population, care setting, and usage domain. This assumption is validated by the clinical validity oracle.

H.2 Key Management Lifecycle

Generation. Keys are generated in an HSM or secure element with a certified random number generator. Key generation is auditable.

Distribution. Signing keys are distributed to authorized components through a secure channel whose protocol is specified and verified.

Rotation. Keys are rotated at defined intervals (e.g., annually) or upon suspicion of compromise. Rotation does not invalidate past legitimate events. The transition from old to new key creates no gap in the audit chain.

Revocation. When a key is compromised, it is revoked. Revocation is propagated to all verifiers. Events signed with a revoked key after the revocation time are treated as invalid; events signed before the revocation time remain valid.

Recovery. If a key is lost, the system has a recovery procedure. The recovery procedure does not allow an attacker to forge events.

H.3 Cross-Compromise Matrix — Formal Statement

For each compromised layer L_i and protected layer L_j, define the guarantee G(i, j) as the set of properties of L_j that remain verifiable when L_i is compromised:

G(i, j) = \begin{cases}
\text{full} & \text{if } L_j \text{ is causally independent of } L_i \text{ and shares no trust root}, \\
\text{conditional} & \text{if } L_j \text{ depends on } L_i \text{ but has an independent verification path}, \\
\text{none} & \text{if } L_j \text{ is fully dependent on } L_i.
\end{cases}

L_i \backslash L_j L0 L1 L2 L3 L4 WD Oracle
L0 — cond cond full cond full full
L1 full — cond full cond full full
L2 full full — full cond full full
L3 full full full — full full full
L4 full full full full — cond cond
WD full full full full full — full
Oracle full full full full cond full —

Critical case. G(L4, L4) is undefined: if L4 is compromised, its decisions cannot be guaranteed authentic. The only protection is rapid key revocation, external audit, and the clinical watchdog.

Shared dependency row. A shared library or runtime that spans L2 and L3 is treated as a separate row. If the shared dependency is compromised, the guarantees of both layers are retracted simultaneously, and the matrix entry is "none" for the affected pair.

H.4 Failure of Trust Assumptions

If assumption A_k fails, the corresponding guarantee is retracted. The system must: (1) detect the failure of A_k if possible; (2) signal the loss of the corresponding guarantee; (3) apply the fallback procedure associated with the lost guarantee; (4) record a TRUST_ASSUMPTION_FAILURE event in the audit log. If the failure cannot be detected, the guarantee is illusory, and the specification must document this limit explicitly. A7 is currently an illusory assumption with a name; the clinical validity oracle is the detection mechanism that makes it non-illusory.

H.5 Clinical Watchdog

The L4 compromise vulnerability is not adequately addressed by post-hoc detection. An attacker who compromises L4 and issues a false P0 alert (or suppresses a true P0 alert) causes clinical action to occur before the external verifier detects the anomaly. A separate, minimal clinical watchdog component monitors L4's outputs for specified anomaly patterns.

Anomaly patterns. A set of formally specified anomaly patterns \mathcal{P}_{\text{anom}} includes: more than n P0 alerts in t seconds from a single L4 instance; a P0 alert for a patient with no corresponding sensor reading; a P0 alert whose clinical urgency field is inconsistent with the patient's recorded context.

Watchdog function. A runtime monitor

\mathcal{W} : \text{OutputStream}(L4) \to \{\text{Normal}, \text{Anomaly}, \text{Critical}\}

that evaluates the output stream of L4 against \mathcal{P}_{\text{anom}}.

Watchdog verification. The watchdog's code must be formally verified against its specification using a method independent of the L4 verification. The verification evidence is included in the safety case.

Isolation. The watchdog runs in a separate execution context (a separate partition in a separation kernel, or a separate hardware module) with no shared memory or IPC channel with L4 except for the monitored output stream.

Fallback authority. The watchdog has the authority to raise a CLINICAL_ANOMALY event and, if the anomaly pattern matches a pre-defined critical pattern, to trigger the local fallback procedure independently of L4. This authority must be explicitly granted and its exercise must be recorded in the audit log. The watchdog's authority is governed by contract C6.

Pattern-set versioning. The anomaly pattern set is versioned. A new pattern version requires independent clinical sign-off. If L4 is compromised, it cannot poison the pattern set because the pattern set is stored outside L4 and is signed by a key not held by L4.

