Appendix I — Freshness Calibration Framework

I.1 Parameters

For each measurement class \tau \in \mathcal{M}, four parameters must be calibrated: the decay function f_\tau : [0, \infty) \to [0, 1]; the abstention threshold \theta_\tau \in (0, 1); the intrinsic quality \sigma_\tau (uncertainty); and the clinical usage set \mathcal{U}_\tau for which the calibration is valid.

I.2 Orthogonality

Age, quality, relevance, and threshold are orthogonal. Formally, for an event e of class \tau and a usage u \in \mathcal{U}_\tau:

\mathrm{usable}(e, u) \iff \mathrm{age}(e, t) \leq f_\tau^{-1}(\theta_\tau(u)) \wedge \sigma_\tau \leq \sigma_{\max}(u) \wedge \mathrm{relevant}(e, u).

The three conditions are independent: an event may satisfy age but not quality, or quality but not relevance.

I.3 Calibration Sources

Each parameter must be calibrated against a documented source. Acceptable sources include: clinical guidelines from recognized societies; peer-reviewed studies of measurement validity; device manufacturer specifications; retrospective analysis of clinical decisions and their timing. The source must be cited with its population, context, and usage domain.

I.4 Calibration Protocol

The calibration is a formal procedure defined by the tuple (population, measurement class, usage domain, clinical guideline source, statistical method, sample size, confidence interval, disagreement resolution protocol). The specification of this protocol is itself a formal object that can be audited.

I.5 Domain Restriction

A calibration is valid only for the domain of its source. A decay function calibrated for capillary glucose in adult diabetics is not automatically valid for continuous glucose monitoring in pediatric patients. The specification requires that every parameter be accompanied by its domain of validity, and that use outside this domain triggers default abstention.

I.6 Worked Illustrative Calibration

The freshness framework is instantiated for capillary glucose in adult diabetics using the following illustrative parameters: decay function f_\tau(t) = e^{-t/15} (time constant 15 minutes), abstention threshold \theta_\tau = 0.5 corresponding to f_\tau^{-1}(0.5) \approx 10.4 minutes, intrinsic quality \sigma_\tau from the device manufacturer specification, and usage set \mathcal{U}_\tau including insulin dosing (where freshness is critical) and trend monitoring (where freshness is less critical). The calibration is flagged as illustrative and must be replaced by clinician-validated values before deployment. It is not derived from any executed calibration study.

I.7 Clinical Workflow Mapping

For each measurement class \tau, the specification lists the clinical workflows that use \tau. A capillary glucose reading may be used for insulin dosing (where freshness is critical) and for trend monitoring (where freshness is less critical). The abstention threshold is workflow-specific. The mapping is a clinical decision, not a formal one, and must be developed with clinical professionals.

I.8 Numerical Values

No numerical values are fixed in this thesis except the volatility window parameters of Section 4 and the illustrative calibration of I.6. The framework specifies the structure of the calibration and the documentation requirements, not the calibrated values themselves. Values must be established by clinical professionals before any real test.

