Appendix J — Failure Mode Catalog: Detailed Specification

J.1 F1 — Network Outage

Scenario. Network outage of 5, 30, or 120 minutes. Required behavior. Display local mode, queue events, trigger fallback procedures according to clinical deadlines. Forbidden behavior. Present a remote alert as delivered without acknowledgement.

Formal specification.

\mathbf{G}(\mathrm{Outage} \to \mathrm{LocalMode} \wedge \mathrm{QueueEvents} \wedge \mathrm{FallbackPerDeadline})

J.2 F2 — Low Bandwidth

Scenario. Useful bandwidth below 100 kbps. Required behavior. Measure available rate, prioritize alerts, send essential metadata before bulky content. Forbidden behavior. Silently degrade a diagnostic image, or announce complete transmission before verification.

J.3 F3 — Duplicated or Out-of-Order Messages

Scenario. Messages duplicated or delivered out of order. Required behavior. Deduplicate by (id, version) at L1, retain provenance, detect conflicts, reconcile events. Forbidden behavior. Execute the same clinical action twice due to retransmission.

Decision-idempotency clause. Event-level dedup at L1 is necessary but not sufficient. The same clinical decision derived from two distinct-but-equivalent events passes event-level dedup. The decision-idempotency invariant at L4 (Appendix D.3) is required.

J.4 F4 — Stale Data

Scenario. Data age exceeds the calibrated threshold. Required behavior. Display data age, apply thresholds specific to each measurement type, abstain if necessary. Forbidden behavior. Present an old measurement as current.

J.5 F5 — Unacknowledged P0 Alert

Scenario. A P0 alert is not acknowledged within the clinical deadline. Required behavior. Trigger the local fallback sequence, then escalate per protocol, tracing each attempt. Forbidden behavior. Assimilate sending to handling, or passively wait for the network.

J.6 F6 — AI Unavailable or Erroneous

Scenario. The AI subsystem is unavailable or produces an erroneous suggestion. Required behavior. Disable dependent functions, signal unavailability, maintain local protections and clinical procedures. Forbidden behavior. Invent a response, bypass rules, or transform a suggestion into a validated decision.

J.7 F7 — Terminal Lost

Scenario. The terminal is lost, destroyed, or without power. Required behavior. Signal loss of service if detectable, protect accessible data, use fallback means, restore from verified backup. Forbidden behavior. Assume unsynchronized data is recoverable, or continue displaying an up-to-date health state without a valid source.

J.8 F8 — Clock Drift

Scenario. The physical clock drifts, jumps, or runs backward. Required behavior. Do not reorder events, do not refresh artificially, do not invalidate a decision due to a temporal jump. Forbidden behavior. Reorder events based on wall-clock order, invalidate a legitimate decision by a temporal jump.

J.9 F9 — Key Rotation or Compromise

Scenario. A signing key is rotated or compromised. Required behavior. Rotate without retroactive invalidation of legitimate events, revoke compromised keys, trace the rotation. Forbidden behavior. Forge a clinical decision using a compromised key, invalidate past legitimate events on rotation.

J.10 F10 — Alarm Flood / Priority Inversion

Scenario. The P0 arrival rate exceeds a defined ceiling \rho_{\max} for a duration T_{\max}. Required behavior. Enter flood state. In flood state, P0 alerts continue to be admitted, P1 alerts are queued with a documented delay bound, and P2/P3 are refused. The flood state is traced and visible to L4. Forbidden behavior. Silently evict P0 or P1, or refuse admission without an explicit loss event.

Exit condition. The transition out of flood state requires the arrival rate to fall below \rho_{\max}/2 for a duration 2T_{\max}.

J.11 F11 — Abstention Storm

Scenario. The default-abstention rate exceeds a defined ceiling over a review period. Required behavior. Raise an ABSTENTION_STORM event. The event triggers a governance review of the calibration parameters. The system does not automatically relax abstention thresholds; the review is a clinical process. Forbidden behavior. Automatically lower abstention thresholds to reduce the abstention rate, or suppress the abstention storm event.

