Appendix K — Adversarial Protocol: Formal Definition

K.1 Adversary Model

Adversary class. The adversary is Dolev–Yao for the network (can eavesdrop, intercept, modify, and inject messages) plus Byzantine for compromised nodes (can behave arbitrarily within their capabilities). The adversary does not break cryptographic primitives or read non-extractable keys.

An adversary is a function

\mathcal{A} : \mathrm{Exec}(\mathcal{S}) \to \mathcal{P}(\mathrm{Act})

that, given a partial execution, returns a set of actions it may inject. The adversary is independent of the declared priority: it may reorder, delay, or drop messages regardless of their priority field.

K.2 Capabilities

The adversary may: inject faults in the Fail alphabet; reorder messages within the transport; delay or drop messages; manipulate wall-clock readings independently of the network; attempt to forge events or decisions; attempt to bypass authorization rules. The adversary may not: violate the hardware trust assumptions without a corresponding capability grant; read non-extractable keys; modify the verification oracle.

K.3 Oracle Separation

The verification oracle is a function

\mathcal{V} : \mathrm{Exec}(\mathcal{S}) \times \Phi \to \{0, 1, \bot_0, \bot_1\}

where \Phi is the set of formal properties, and the four-valued result distinguishes: property verified, property violated, inconclusive with no violation found, inconclusive with violation possible. The oracle does not share code, memory, or execution context with the system.

K.4 Clinical Validity Oracle

The clinical validity oracle challenges the authorization rules themselves. It does not verify LTL formulas. It audits the clinical provenance of the rules: which guidelines were used, which populations were studied, which usage domains were assumed. The oracle's verdict is a precondition for any claim of clinical safety.

Oracle separation. No personnel who author authorization rules may sit on the oracle that validates them. The oracle's membership is documented and its independence is auditable.

Disagreement protocol. When clinical professionals disagree on whether a rule's domain of validity applies, the system records a CLINICAL_DISAGREEMENT event and escalates to a designated clinical authority. The resolution is recorded with its provenance.

Oracle compromise. The oracle is a component in the trust model. If the oracle is compromised, the guarantees it provides are retracted. The cross-compromise matrix includes an oracle row and column.

Periodic re-validation. Authorization rules are re-validated at defined intervals against updated guidelines. A rule whose validation has expired is treated as OutOfDomain.

K.5 Test Loop (Specified, Not Executed)

The test loop is specified as follows: (1) fix a seed; (2) instantiate the system with L1, L4, audit, vector clocks, and \varphi; (3) inject a fault F_i via the adversarial scheduler; (4) observe the state after stabilization; (5) verify P1–P4 with the independent oracle; (6) produce a machine-readable report.

Epistemic status. This loop has not been executed. No seed has been fixed, no fault has been injected, no state has been observed, and no report has been produced. The loop is a specification of how the falsifiability claim would be tested; it is not evidence that the claim has been tested. The falsifiability wager is therefore unpaid. This is a named limitation, not a hidden gap.

K.6 Result Classification

Each result is classified as: confirmed counter-example (a reproducible trace violates a formal property); property not demonstrated (model, hypotheses, or oracle insufficient to conclude); property satisfied in the model (no counter-example found in the explored space). The third category does not constitute a proof. Ten million successful tests do not constitute an exhaustive proof if the system retains unexplored behaviors.

Value Meaning
True Product empty: no counter-example found
False A reproducible counter-example was found
Inconclusive-true Model does not allow conclusion, but no violation found
Inconclusive-false Model does not allow conclusion, and violating behaviors are possible

Positioning against 3-valued LTL. The four-valued verdict overlaps established 3-valued LTL under abstraction and runtime-verification literature. It is a sound adoption, not a novel contribution.

K.7 Reproducibility

A scenario that fails once must be replayable identically from its seed. A test that is not reproducible is not a test. The report contains the seed, configuration, trace, model version, counter-example, and oracle verdict. This requirement is specified; it has not been exercised because no test has been run.

