Appendix M — Regulatory Applicability Analysis (United States)

M.1 Applicability Framework

Regulatory applicability depends on three parameters: the destination of the product; the targeted market; and the intended clinical usage. A system intended for a research pilot is not automatically subject to the regulatory regime of a jurisdiction where it is not intended to be marketed.

M.2 FDA Quality Management System Regulation

The FDA Quality Management System Regulation (QMSR) became effective on February 2, 2026. It amends the FDA's device current good manufacturing practice requirements in 21 CFR Part 820 and incorporates ISO 13485:2016 by reference, supplemented by FDA-specific requirements. The QMSR replaced the Quality System Regulation (QSR) and harmonized the FDA's device quality system requirements with the international standard ISO 13485:2016. A system intended for a U.S. pilot is subject to the QMSR if it is a finished device intended for commercial distribution. A research prototype not intended for commercial distribution is not automatically subject to the QMSR, but the intended use must be documented.

M.3 IEC 62304

IEC 62304:2006 (amended 2015) defines the life cycle requirements for medical device software. The set of processes, activities, and tasks described in this standard establishes a common framework for medical device software life cycle processes. The second edition is an ongoing normative project; its two-level rigor scheme (Level I/II) replacing Classes A/B/C is stable across drafts, and this thesis notes it without citing the second edition as a definitive requirement. Formal methods are permitted as a verification technique under Class B and Class C. The Lean 4 and UPPAAL artifacts of this thesis are verification evidence under that standard for the components they cover.

M.4 ISO 14971

ISO 14971:2019 specifies risk management for medical devices. ISO 14971 acts as the foundational layer, defining the overall risk management process that all risks, including those related to cybersecurity, must follow. It clarifies the core "what" and "why" of risk management. Cybersecurity is now within the scope of ISO 14971, treating loss of confidentiality, integrity, or availability as a potential source of harm. Under the EU Medical Device Regulation and ISO 14971:2019+A11:2021, software and connected devices must show cybersecurity risk assessments linked to patient outcomes.

M.5 IEC 81001-5-1

IEC 81001-5-1:2021 defines life cycle requirements for health software cybersecurity, supporting conformance to IEC 62443-4-1. The FDA recognizes IEC 81001-5-1 as a consensus standard. It is the missing security-engineering frame that ISO 14971 alone does not provide. ISO 14971 is a risk-management frame; IEC 81001-5-1 is a security-engineering frame. Both are required.

M.6 FDA Premarket Cybersecurity Guidance

The FDA issued revised cybersecurity premarket guidance in 2026, aligned with the QMSR. The guidance recommends cybersecurity device design, labeling, and documentation for premarket submissions, including threat modeling, update and patch management, and a software bill of materials (SBOM). For a U.S. pilot, the cybersecurity documentation must be part of the premarket submission if the device has cybersecurity risk.

M.7 Interoperability

A clinical continuity system that does not position its L0–L3 interface contracts against HL7 FHIR leaves its abstractions floating free of the protocols hospital systems speak. L3 is positioned against FHIR subscription and communication resources. The mapping from SAT-MED events to FHIR resources is part of the interface specification.

M.8 Regulatory Stop Criterion

The regulatory stop criterion is: the analysis must be documented, the applicable framework must be identified, and the analysis must conclude that the system can legally be deployed for its intended use. An analysis that concludes "we cannot determine whether this is legal" is a stop condition, not a pass.

