Appendix N — Stop Criteria: Formal Statement

N.1 Blocking Conditions

Six conditions block progression:

N.1.1 Critical safety failure.

\exists t : \mathrm{AssertStable}(p, t) \wedge (\mathrm{Unknown}(p, t) \vee \mathrm{Stale}(p, t))

or

\exists t : \mathrm{UnauthorizedAction}(t).

N.1.2 Escalation failure.

\exists a : \mathrm{Alert}(a, P0) \wedge \mathrm{Handled}(a) \wedge \neg \mathrm{ValidClinicalAck}(a).

N.1.3 Integrity failure.

\exists s : \mathrm{Synchronization}(s) \wedge (\mathrm{SilentLoss}(s) \vee \mathrm{DuplicatedAction}(s) \vee \mathrm{UnjustifiedOverwrite}(s)).

N.1.4 Recovery failure.

\exists c : \neg \mathrm{DetectedCapacityLoss}(c) \vee \neg \mathrm{TestedFallback}(c).

N.1.5 Auditability failure.

\exists d : \mathrm{ValidatedDecision}(d) \wedge \neg \mathrm{Reconstructible}(d),

or reconstruction depends on the system that produced the decision.

N.1.6 Regulatory failure.

\neg \mathrm{DocumentedRegulatoryAnalysis}.

N.2 Rule

A single confirmed critical failure blocks progression until root-cause analysis, correction, and repetition of the relevant tests. No favorable statistical average compensates for a serious clinical failure.

N.3 Recovery from Blocking

Blocking is not permanent abandonment. Progression resumes when: the root cause is identified; a correction is specified; the correction is applied; the relevant tests are repeated and pass; the correction is documented in the specification.

N.4 Named Stop Conditions Arising from This Thesis

The following are named as blocking conditions that have not been resolved:

· Clinical companion protocol. The human-side protocol after FAILED is unfilled. Its unfilled state blocks deployment.
· Clinical validity oracle at scale. The oracle is specified but not instantiated for a realistic rule set. Its absence blocks the claim that A7 is non-illusory.
· Vector clock equivalence test. The equivalence test is specified but not executed. Its unexecuted state blocks adoption of the truncated mode for deployment.
· Composite formalization. The full six-layer system with a realistic clinical rule set is not instantiated. Its absence bounds the executability claim.
· Adversarial runs. The test loop is specified but not executed. Its unexecuted state leaves the falsifiability wager unpaid.
· Acceptance thresholds. No threshold has been fixed because no experiment has been designed. Its unfixed state blocks any claim of measured performance.

