Appendix O — Glossary

Abstention. A clinical safety decision by which the system refrains from producing a suggestion or decision when the input is stale, out of domain, or otherwise insufficient.

Adversarial oracle. An independent verification function that reimplements the invariants from their specification alone, without reusing the system's code.

Bounded volatility window. The maximum theoretical data loss under a power failure for a given event class.

Causal order. The partial order on events induced by vector clocks, distinguishing anteriority, posteriority, concurrency, and equality/anomaly.

Clinical companion protocol. A structured artifact that specifies the human-side escalation chain, roles, and fallback duties after a terminal machine failure. Its unfilled state is a named stop condition.

Clinical urgency. The priority of an event for clinical action, distinct from transmission priority.

Clinical validity oracle. An independent function that audits the clinical provenance of authorization rules: which guidelines were used, which populations were studied, which usage domains were assumed. It is a socio-technical component with separation of personnel, a disagreement protocol, and a place in the trust model.

Cross-compromise matrix. A matrix specifying, for each pair (compromised layer, protected layer), the guarantees that remain under stated trust assumptions.

Decision-idempotency invariant. The L4 invariant that a clinical decision already executed is not re-executed when the same decision is derived from a distinct-but-equivalent event.

Durable. The state of a transaction whose commit marker has been successfully persisted according to the required primitive for its priority class.

Escalation automaton. A phase automaton governing the handling of an alert, with distinct phases and deadlines for transport acknowledgement, local human acknowledgement, backup channel activation, and remote clinical acknowledgement.

Flood state. A degraded mode entered when the P0 arrival rate exceeds a defined ceiling for a defined duration. P0 alerts continue to be admitted; P1 alerts are queued with a documented delay bound; P2/P3 are refused.

Indeterminate. The state of a transaction whose BEGIN record survived but whose COMMIT record did not, and whose final state cannot be determined without external information.

Interval Tree Clocks (ITC). A causality tracking mechanism that generalizes both version vectors and vector clocks, designed for dynamic systems with no global identifiers. Not adopted by default; considered as a candidate optimization subject to the equivalence criterion.

Morphism \varphi. The monotone non-injective map from clinical urgency classes to transmission priority classes.

Non-evictive. A property of a queue class whose elements can only be removed by confirmed delivery, authorized decision, or documented deadline expiration.

Projection. The restriction of a global action to a single layer of the stratified LTS.

Reconciliation. The function that fuses local and remote states after reconnection, idempotent and commutative on non-conflicting events.

Signed checkpoint. A periodic signature over the current log state, providing authenticity and exhaustiveness in addition to integrity.

Stratified LTS. A labeled transition system whose state space is a Cartesian product of layer state spaces, with interface contracts constraining the authorized projections.

Therac-25 precedent. The 1985–1987 accidents in which a software race condition in a radiation therapy machine caused massive overdoses. The investigation established that the manufacturer did not consider the software design during its assessment of failure modes, focusing on hardware and asserting the software was free of bugs. The precedent demonstrates that formal correctness of software does not imply clinical correctness of the system.

Timed automaton. A finite-state automaton extended with real-valued clocks, guards, resets, and invariants, used for verification of deadline-bearing properties.

Truncated vector clock. A bounded-width vector clock with explicit truncation semantics, used in the transport layer when the node count exceeds k_{\max}.

Trust assumption. An explicit hypothesis (hardware root of trust, protected keys, independent authorization policy, verifier separation, physical independence of checkpoints, clinical validity of L4 rules) under which a guarantee holds.

Vector clock. A vector in \mathbb{N}^k assigned to each event, encoding the causal history of the event across k nodes.

Watchdog. A minimal, independently verified, isolated component that monitors L4's outputs for anomaly patterns and, on a pre-defined critical pattern, triggers the local fallback procedure independently of L4. Governed by contract C6.


